(Problem: Select the semantically appropriate quantifier to convey the intended) Tj
T*
(scientific meaning:) Tj
T*
(Sentence 1: "Unfortunately, [few / a few] organisms survived the radiation pulse,) Tj
T*
(causing the experimental trial to fail.") Tj
T*
(- Solution: FEW. \(Negative connotation 'almost none' required because the trial) Tj
T*
(failed\).) Tj
T*
(Sentence 2: "Although resources were scarce, [little / a little] biomass was harvested,) Tj
T*
(providing sufficient material for initial PCR testing.") Tj
T*
(- Solution: A LITTLE. \(Positive connotation 'some quantity' required because it) Tj
T*
(was sufficient for PCR\).) Tj
T*
(Sentence 3: "Because [little / a little] contamination was detected in the culture, the) Tj
T*
(entire fermentation batch was discarded.") Tj
T*
(- Solution: A LITTLE. \('A little contamination' is a positive non-zero quantity) Tj
T*
(sufficient to trigger batch rejection\).) Tj
T*
(Sentence 4: "Only [few / a few] researchers in the department possess certification for) Tj
T*
(handling Level 3 biohazards.") Tj
T*
(- Solution: A FEW. \('Only a few' denotes a small positive subset\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([EXERCISE 4: Distributive Determiners: Each, Every, Either, Neither]) Tj
T*
(Problem: Fill in the blanks with Each, Every, Either, or Neither:) Tj
T*
(Sentence 1: "[ _________ ] of the two bioreactor ports is fitted with a sterile membrane) Tj
T*
(filter.") Tj
T*
(- Solution: EACH or EITHER. \('of the two' limits the set to exactly two; 'Each') Tj
T*
(means both individually; 'Either' means one or the other\).) Tj
T*
(Sentence 2: "The automated robotic arm pipetted 10 microL into [ _________ ] well of the) Tj
T*
(96-well microplate.") Tj
T*
(- Solution: EVERY. \('Every' refers to all items in a large structured collection\).) Tj
T*
(Sentence 3: "[ _________ ] of the two hypotheses was supported by the experimental data,) Tj
T*
(forcing the team to develop a third model.") Tj
T*
(- Solution: NEITHER. \(Negative selection from two options\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 4.4 University Review & Examination Questions) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Q1: Formulate the fundamental phonetic rule governing the distribution of) Tj
T*
(indefinite articles 'a' and 'an' with five contrasting examples.) Tj
T*
(Answer: The assignment of 'a' vs. 'an' is governed entirely by the initial phonetic) Tj
T*
(sound of the word that immediately follows, rather than its orthographic spelling. 'A') Tj
T*
(precedes words beginning with consonant sounds \(including semi-vowels /j/ and /w/\). 'An') Tj
T*
(precedes words beginning with vowel sounds \(/a/, /e/, /i/, /o/, /u/\). Five contrasting) Tj
T*
(pairs: \(1\) 'A university' \(/j/ glide\) vs. 'An umbrella' \(vowel /u/\); \(2\) 'A European') Tj
T*
(\(/j/ glide\) vs. 'An error' \(vowel /e/\); \(3\) 'A hospital' \(pronounced 'h'\) vs. 'An hour') Tj
T*
(\(silent 'h', initial vowel\); \(4\) 'A one-step protocol' \(consonant /w/\) vs. 'An optical) Tj
T*
(sensor' \(vowel /o/\); \(5\) 'A PhD student' \(consonant /p/\) vs. 'An MBA candidate' \(vowel) Tj
T*
(/e/ as in 'em'\).) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Differentiate between 'Few' vs. 'A Few' and 'Little' vs. 'A Little', detailing) Tj
T*
